WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.492 --> 00:00:03.700
GUSTAVO FRANCO: First, before
we jump into the topic,

00:00:03.700 --> 00:00:04.350
so who am I?

00:00:04.350 --> 00:00:07.400
I'm Gustavo Franco, aka Stratus.

00:00:07.400 --> 00:00:09.483
I've been with Google
for roughly 11 years now.

00:00:09.483 --> 00:00:11.400
I work on the Customer
Reliability Engineering

00:00:11.400 --> 00:00:13.670
team, which is part of the
SRE organization, which

00:00:13.670 --> 00:00:15.990
is partially responsible
for this launch

00:00:15.990 --> 00:00:18.323
if you're watching here, if
you're not watching at home.

00:00:21.175 --> 00:00:23.550
Let's go over some assumptions
before we jump right into,

00:00:23.550 --> 00:00:27.630
like do you have an SRE team,
how to get to an SRE team.

00:00:27.630 --> 00:00:29.340
I have a couple
assumptions, and you

00:00:29.340 --> 00:00:31.050
can correct me if I'm wrong.

00:00:31.050 --> 00:00:34.740
I think, hopefully, most
of you here are already

00:00:34.740 --> 00:00:38.370
familiar with what SRE is.

00:00:38.370 --> 00:00:43.170
And you probably at least
have a vague understanding

00:00:43.170 --> 00:00:45.660
of the difference
between SRE and DevOps.

00:00:45.660 --> 00:00:48.113
If you don't, follow
that link in there

00:00:48.113 --> 00:00:49.530
to get more
information about what

00:00:49.530 --> 00:00:51.695
we think of the difference
between SRE and DevOps.

00:00:51.695 --> 00:00:53.070
The long story
short here, and we

00:00:53.070 --> 00:00:55.640
had a presentation
earlier at this conference

00:00:55.640 --> 00:00:58.500
from Seth Vargo, one of the
Developer Relations folks,

00:00:58.500 --> 00:01:00.670
about the difference
between SRE and DevOps,

00:01:00.670 --> 00:01:03.330
which I recommend you to watch
if you haven't watched it yet.

00:01:03.330 --> 00:01:08.490
The gist of it is we say SRE
is an implementation of DevOps.

00:01:08.490 --> 00:01:11.010
A lot more information for
you if you follow that link.

00:01:11.010 --> 00:01:14.190
And the third assumption
that I have is there's

00:01:14.190 --> 00:01:16.110
some leadership buy-in.

00:01:16.110 --> 00:01:20.680
VPs, C-level executives
have some buy-in.

00:01:20.680 --> 00:01:22.530
Either if you're a
manager or if you're

00:01:22.530 --> 00:01:24.560
an individual contributor,
try to implement,

00:01:24.560 --> 00:01:26.670
adopt SRE best practices,
and implement an SRE

00:01:26.670 --> 00:01:29.088
team at your company.

00:01:29.088 --> 00:01:30.380
People generally agree with it.

00:01:34.610 --> 00:01:37.850
So why are we here?

00:01:37.850 --> 00:01:42.310
So first, this can be seen as
a guided tour of the Google SRE

00:01:42.310 --> 00:01:44.277
workbook.

00:01:44.277 --> 00:01:46.360
If you haven't read it
yet, we have a copy online.

00:01:46.360 --> 00:01:47.995
I'll have a link at the end.

00:01:47.995 --> 00:01:49.370
We also have some
copies for you.

00:01:49.370 --> 00:01:52.030
So after this talk,
towards the end,

00:01:52.030 --> 00:01:57.200
you can actually get a copy
from us as part of this talk.

00:01:57.200 --> 00:01:58.930
But first things first.

00:01:58.930 --> 00:02:00.940
We're here because
I'm going to guide you

00:02:00.940 --> 00:02:05.530
through some chapters of the
workbook in order to get there,

00:02:05.530 --> 00:02:08.289
in order to get to
your first SRE team.

00:02:08.289 --> 00:02:10.780
The second reason
why we're here is

00:02:10.780 --> 00:02:12.610
I'll give you concrete examples.

00:02:12.610 --> 00:02:14.860
One thing that we
get feedback often

00:02:14.860 --> 00:02:18.970
about when we talk about SRE
with customers like yourselves,

00:02:18.970 --> 00:02:21.370
is, OK, I understand
the practice.

00:02:21.370 --> 00:02:23.560
I heard about SLIs and SLOs.

00:02:23.560 --> 00:02:25.120
How do I really do it?

00:02:25.120 --> 00:02:28.060
How do I get from quote,
unquote "nothing,"

00:02:28.060 --> 00:02:29.860
or an operational
team or a developer

00:02:29.860 --> 00:02:33.643
team that wants to adopt
SRE to actual concrete,

00:02:33.643 --> 00:02:34.310
tangible action?

00:02:34.310 --> 00:02:35.893
So we're going to
cover some examples,

00:02:35.893 --> 00:02:38.590
some concrete examples there.

00:02:38.590 --> 00:02:41.507
Last but not least, we're
here so we can use this

00:02:41.507 --> 00:02:42.590
as some kind of benchmark.

00:02:42.590 --> 00:02:43.965
So if you have
SRE teams already,

00:02:43.965 --> 00:02:45.900
you can kind of
compare notes with us.

00:02:45.900 --> 00:02:48.910
We'd like to hear your feedback
right after the presentation

00:02:48.910 --> 00:02:51.640
through questions, or
just we can go in the back

00:02:51.640 --> 00:02:55.000
and have a
conversation about it.

00:02:55.000 --> 00:02:55.560
Cool.

00:02:55.560 --> 00:02:58.240
So first things first.

00:02:58.240 --> 00:03:01.600
I will go through with
you just getting started.

00:03:01.600 --> 00:03:05.130
So you're sitting here, and
you don't have an SRE team yet,

00:03:05.130 --> 00:03:07.710
and you're really interested
in adopting SRE best practices,

00:03:07.710 --> 00:03:08.790
and you have an SRE team.

00:03:08.790 --> 00:03:11.370
We're going to go through that.

00:03:11.370 --> 00:03:14.770
I'll also cover different
SRE implementations,

00:03:14.770 --> 00:03:17.340
so different flavors of SRE.

00:03:17.340 --> 00:03:20.490
And I'll also guide you
through common SRE practices

00:03:20.490 --> 00:03:21.930
by level of maturity.

00:03:21.930 --> 00:03:23.190
This is totally arbitrary.

00:03:23.190 --> 00:03:25.050
I made this up, right?

00:03:25.050 --> 00:03:25.950
If you say--

00:03:25.950 --> 00:03:27.900
I go through advanced
and you're like,

00:03:27.900 --> 00:03:29.490
no, my team is doing
half of advanced

00:03:29.490 --> 00:03:31.950
and half of beginner,
where am I at?

00:03:31.950 --> 00:03:33.520
I don't have an answer for you.

00:03:33.520 --> 00:03:35.280
I just have rough
guidelines in terms

00:03:35.280 --> 00:03:37.020
of what I see beginner
teams at Google

00:03:37.020 --> 00:03:42.650
doing after intermediate
teams and advanced teams.

00:03:42.650 --> 00:03:46.290
And then, of course,
what we should do next.

00:03:46.290 --> 00:03:47.720
So if you're just
getting started,

00:03:47.720 --> 00:03:50.017
first recommendation
is establish

00:03:50.017 --> 00:03:51.350
service-level objectives, right?

00:03:51.350 --> 00:03:54.170
So I won't get into
details of what

00:03:54.170 --> 00:03:57.470
this means, but,
of course, you can

00:03:57.470 --> 00:04:00.170
see the link to the
chapter of the book

00:04:00.170 --> 00:04:03.410
as I mentioned before as
you access the presentation.

00:04:03.410 --> 00:04:04.910
You can get a free
copy of the book,

00:04:04.910 --> 00:04:08.540
or you can go to google.com/SRE
to read a chapter.

00:04:08.540 --> 00:04:10.400
One thing I will say
about establishing SLOs

00:04:10.400 --> 00:04:12.275
and why that's so
important if you're getting

00:04:12.275 --> 00:04:14.960
started, even if you don't
have an SRE team yet,

00:04:14.960 --> 00:04:18.019
is how is it that
you're going to justify

00:04:18.019 --> 00:04:21.980
prioritizing stability
over feature velocity

00:04:21.980 --> 00:04:25.100
if you don't know where your
bar for reliability is at?

00:04:25.100 --> 00:04:28.167
Your bar has to be
anything over than 100%.

00:04:28.167 --> 00:04:29.750
My colleague Jesus
was just explaining

00:04:29.750 --> 00:04:32.360
the proofs presentation,
so hopefully some of you

00:04:32.360 --> 00:04:34.940
caught that.

00:04:34.940 --> 00:04:36.830
Second thing that we
recommend folks to do

00:04:36.830 --> 00:04:40.400
is adopt blameless postmortems.

00:04:40.400 --> 00:04:43.048
It's very easy in
some companies.

00:04:43.048 --> 00:04:45.590
It's very easy in some companies
for people to buy into that.

00:04:45.590 --> 00:04:47.465
In some other companies,
people are like, no.

00:04:47.465 --> 00:04:49.190
If someone makes
a honest mistake,

00:04:49.190 --> 00:04:52.850
I still think we should quote,
unquote "punish" them somehow.

00:04:52.850 --> 00:04:54.330
If you are one of
those companies,

00:04:54.330 --> 00:04:56.122
if you're working in
one of those companies

00:04:56.122 --> 00:04:58.123
where people want to
quote, unquote punish folks

00:04:58.123 --> 00:04:59.540
because they make
honest mistakes,

00:04:59.540 --> 00:05:01.610
it's really hard to adopt
blameless postmortems.

00:05:01.610 --> 00:05:04.190
So if you cannot
change that culture,

00:05:04.190 --> 00:05:07.250
maybe SRE is not for you,
is not for your company.

00:05:07.250 --> 00:05:08.700
Maybe it's not
going to help you.

00:05:08.700 --> 00:05:09.940
It's not going to work.

00:05:09.940 --> 00:05:12.260
So back to you one
thing to consider.

00:05:12.260 --> 00:05:14.120
Establish SLIs and SLOs.

00:05:14.120 --> 00:05:16.123
Establish blameless postmortems.

00:05:16.123 --> 00:05:17.540
Establishing
blameless postmortems

00:05:17.540 --> 00:05:18.800
is all about culture.

00:05:18.800 --> 00:05:20.270
It's not technical.

00:05:20.270 --> 00:05:22.970
And if the company
culture is not ready,

00:05:22.970 --> 00:05:25.670
you're going to have a
problem adopting your SRE best

00:05:25.670 --> 00:05:28.460
practices, basically.

00:05:28.460 --> 00:05:31.490
The third one is, once you
have blameless postmortems,

00:05:31.490 --> 00:05:35.090
establish a process to
manage production incidents.

00:05:35.090 --> 00:05:35.990
Why a process?

00:05:35.990 --> 00:05:38.130
Well, you don't want people
to be paged at 3:00 AM.

00:05:38.130 --> 00:05:43.310
You don't want people being
paged in all the weekends,

00:05:43.310 --> 00:05:45.590
and they don't know what
to do with that pager.

00:05:45.590 --> 00:05:48.770
So if you have SLIs, SLOs, you
have blameless postmortems,

00:05:48.770 --> 00:05:51.600
document your process
to respond to pages.

00:05:51.600 --> 00:05:55.130
We will often see SRE
teams, SRE functions,

00:05:55.130 --> 00:05:58.023
where people have adopted SLIs,
SLOs, they write postmortem.

00:05:58.023 --> 00:05:59.940
They have the practice
of writing postmortems.

00:05:59.940 --> 00:06:02.023
When people get paged,
they don't know what to do.

00:06:02.023 --> 00:06:03.440
So train your folks.

00:06:03.440 --> 00:06:04.910
Establish a process.

00:06:04.910 --> 00:06:07.610
And back to this chapter
covers more details

00:06:07.610 --> 00:06:11.400
of things to do there.

00:06:11.400 --> 00:06:12.460
All right.

00:06:12.460 --> 00:06:14.710
We covered things that if
you're just getting started.

00:06:14.710 --> 00:06:15.960
So three things.

00:06:15.960 --> 00:06:19.590
But I understand not every
SRE team is created equal.

00:06:19.590 --> 00:06:21.960
Maybe you're able to
do two out of three,

00:06:21.960 --> 00:06:24.030
and it might be OK,
except for maybe blameless

00:06:24.030 --> 00:06:26.113
postmortems because you
might have cultural issues

00:06:26.113 --> 00:06:26.820
at your company.

00:06:26.820 --> 00:06:29.280
Let's talk about why not every
SRE team is created equal.

00:06:32.270 --> 00:06:35.630
I see commonly five to
six SRE different flavors,

00:06:35.630 --> 00:06:38.030
different team
implementations, and that's

00:06:38.030 --> 00:06:40.130
where people get quote,
unquote "a little lost."

00:06:40.130 --> 00:06:42.920
Because they say, oh,
I do SRE, but I don't

00:06:42.920 --> 00:06:44.810
have SRE quite like Google.

00:06:44.810 --> 00:06:46.280
And I say, well,
how do you do SRE?

00:06:46.280 --> 00:06:48.140
And after they tell
me how they do SRE.

00:06:48.140 --> 00:06:50.390
And I say, no, yeah, we
have several teams at Google

00:06:50.390 --> 00:06:51.950
that are just like yours.

00:06:51.950 --> 00:06:54.560
It's just Google has an
entire SRE organization.

00:06:54.560 --> 00:06:56.690
We have very different
implementations of SRE

00:06:56.690 --> 00:06:57.790
inside of Google.

00:06:57.790 --> 00:06:59.540
So I'll guide you
through some of the ones

00:06:59.540 --> 00:07:02.090
that I've seen both at
Google and outside of Google.

00:07:02.090 --> 00:07:04.700
The first one is I think
is interesting to highlight

00:07:04.700 --> 00:07:06.650
is consulting.

00:07:06.650 --> 00:07:10.340
So consulting is--
you may have a couple

00:07:10.340 --> 00:07:15.060
SREs that are consulting with
developers on their design.

00:07:15.060 --> 00:07:18.398
They might be consulting
on configuration changes.

00:07:18.398 --> 00:07:20.440
They might be consulting
on the instant response,

00:07:20.440 --> 00:07:23.300
on blameless postmortems,
on defining SLIs and SLOs.

00:07:23.300 --> 00:07:25.520
But at the end of
the day, consultants

00:07:25.520 --> 00:07:28.105
don't tend to get
quote, unquote,

00:07:28.105 --> 00:07:29.480
their "hands dirty"
with changing

00:07:29.480 --> 00:07:31.940
configuration, changing code.

00:07:31.940 --> 00:07:34.200
That's why they're
called consultants.

00:07:34.200 --> 00:07:34.700
This is OK.

00:07:34.700 --> 00:07:35.990
If this is all you
can afford, if that's

00:07:35.990 --> 00:07:37.560
what you want to
implement in your company,

00:07:37.560 --> 00:07:38.660
you can have an SRE team.

00:07:38.660 --> 00:07:40.965
Your first SRE team
can be of consultants.

00:07:40.965 --> 00:07:42.590
We'll talk about some
of the main risks

00:07:42.590 --> 00:07:45.440
I've seen in consultant
teams in a moment.

00:07:48.000 --> 00:07:50.390
Second implementation--
embedded teams.

00:07:50.390 --> 00:07:52.760
So embedded teams, you have--

00:07:52.760 --> 00:07:54.600
generally, you don't
embed an entire team.

00:07:54.600 --> 00:07:56.200
You embed individuals of a team.

00:07:56.200 --> 00:07:58.610
So you may have a team
of five folks, five SREs.

00:07:58.610 --> 00:08:00.600
One, two, three
are embedded with--

00:08:00.600 --> 00:08:01.100
each, right?

00:08:01.100 --> 00:08:03.980
One or two each are embedded
with different developers

00:08:03.980 --> 00:08:05.720
working with them, right?

00:08:05.720 --> 00:08:07.500
We also have those at Google.

00:08:07.500 --> 00:08:09.110
So if you have this at your
company and you're saying,

00:08:09.110 --> 00:08:10.610
I'm not doing SRE
quite like Google,

00:08:10.610 --> 00:08:12.140
yes, you are, and it's OK.

00:08:12.140 --> 00:08:13.610
So that's one implementation.

00:08:13.610 --> 00:08:14.850
It's totally acceptable.

00:08:14.850 --> 00:08:16.100
That could be your first team.

00:08:16.100 --> 00:08:19.550
You could have one, two
folks to be embedded there.

00:08:19.550 --> 00:08:22.100
Generally, the difference
between embedded and consulting

00:08:22.100 --> 00:08:23.900
is we don't see--

00:08:23.900 --> 00:08:26.450
we do see embedded folks
going hands-on, changing code

00:08:26.450 --> 00:08:26.990
and config.

00:08:26.990 --> 00:08:29.510
They may or may not be
on call with the team.

00:08:29.510 --> 00:08:31.920
But back to it's
your implementation.

00:08:31.920 --> 00:08:32.662
You define it.

00:08:32.662 --> 00:08:33.620
You define the details.

00:08:35.792 --> 00:08:38.250
The third and the fourth one--
they kind of bundle together

00:08:38.250 --> 00:08:39.625
because they tend
to be similar--

00:08:39.625 --> 00:08:42.210
are the tools or
the infra SRE team.

00:08:42.210 --> 00:08:44.760
So the tools or the infra SRE
team is this SRE team that

00:08:44.760 --> 00:08:47.100
specializes on either
a set of tools,

00:08:47.100 --> 00:08:50.400
so they build tooling for the
developers, for the company--

00:08:50.400 --> 00:08:53.220
reliability tools, maybe
monitor-related tools,

00:08:53.220 --> 00:08:56.910
maybe the SLI/SLO-related
tools, instant response,

00:08:56.910 --> 00:08:58.590
blameless postmortem tools.

00:08:58.590 --> 00:09:01.320
Or they could be an
infrastructure SRE team,

00:09:01.320 --> 00:09:02.970
where they're responsible for--

00:09:02.970 --> 00:09:04.470
let's say now you're
running on GKE,

00:09:04.470 --> 00:09:07.080
or you have your own
Kubernetes cluster.

00:09:07.080 --> 00:09:08.660
So that infrastructure
SRE team may

00:09:08.660 --> 00:09:11.400
be responsible for the quote,
unquote "infrastructure,"

00:09:11.400 --> 00:09:12.690
not the application.

00:09:15.390 --> 00:09:20.375
Slice is when you get a
slice of your problem space

00:09:20.375 --> 00:09:21.150
at the company.

00:09:21.150 --> 00:09:22.358
It could be a product, right?

00:09:22.358 --> 00:09:24.330
A single product is
a slice, or a set

00:09:24.330 --> 00:09:26.170
of products, a product area.

00:09:26.170 --> 00:09:27.960
So those are SRE
teams that they are

00:09:27.960 --> 00:09:32.460
responsible for a
slice of the problem.

00:09:32.460 --> 00:09:34.242
Kitchen sink, or
everything, tends

00:09:34.242 --> 00:09:35.700
to be the most
common that I see in

00:09:35.700 --> 00:09:38.060
other companies, where in the--

00:09:38.060 --> 00:09:40.560
especially when they're at the
beginning of the SRE journey,

00:09:40.560 --> 00:09:41.910
that's their first SRE team.

00:09:41.910 --> 00:09:44.620
So they tend to name the
team after the company.

00:09:44.620 --> 00:09:48.330
So if you're a company and you
name your SRE team Company SRE,

00:09:48.330 --> 00:09:50.700
and I ask, what is this
SRE team responsible for?

00:09:50.700 --> 00:09:51.958
You say everything.

00:09:51.958 --> 00:09:54.000
That's what I would consider
to be a kitchen sink

00:09:54.000 --> 00:09:55.153
implementation.

00:09:57.685 --> 00:09:59.560
I'll leave it up there
a little bit because I

00:09:59.560 --> 00:10:02.340
see a couple folks taking
photos of the implementations.

00:10:02.340 --> 00:10:05.140
We'll cover main
risks and a little bit

00:10:05.140 --> 00:10:08.420
about on-call and team size,
which people ask me quite a bit

00:10:08.420 --> 00:10:08.920
about.

00:10:14.080 --> 00:10:17.788
Before we get into that, one way
to scale-- people ask me often,

00:10:17.788 --> 00:10:19.330
OK, what is the
right implementation?

00:10:19.330 --> 00:10:20.350
What is the right mix?

00:10:20.350 --> 00:10:21.810
It depends.

00:10:21.810 --> 00:10:24.340
One thing that I've
seen folks scaling, some

00:10:24.340 --> 00:10:26.330
of these implementations
in place,

00:10:26.330 --> 00:10:28.540
especially tools in for
a slice and kitchen sink,

00:10:28.540 --> 00:10:30.430
is tiers of service.

00:10:30.430 --> 00:10:31.960
What does that mean?

00:10:31.960 --> 00:10:35.740
You might tell your
developers, hey, you know what?

00:10:35.740 --> 00:10:37.720
I cannot be on call.

00:10:37.720 --> 00:10:40.840
I cannot do project work
on everything that we have

00:10:40.840 --> 00:10:42.230
in the company.

00:10:42.230 --> 00:10:44.850
And even though I'm responsible
for a slice or tools or infra,

00:10:44.850 --> 00:10:45.480
it's too much.

00:10:45.480 --> 00:10:46.270
We have too many tools.

00:10:46.270 --> 00:10:47.853
We have too much
infrastructure, or we

00:10:47.853 --> 00:10:49.630
have-- the slice is too big.

00:10:49.630 --> 00:10:52.870
So I've seen people
adding tiers of service.

00:10:52.870 --> 00:10:54.625
So you can imagine
a first tier where

00:10:54.625 --> 00:10:56.607
you say, I'm just
going to do consulting,

00:10:56.607 --> 00:10:58.690
a second tier, where you
do project work, and then

00:10:58.690 --> 00:11:00.850
eventually, a third tier,
where you do on-call.

00:11:00.850 --> 00:11:02.720
Those tiers are
defined per company,

00:11:02.720 --> 00:11:05.950
so you can write down, as
part of your team charter

00:11:05.950 --> 00:11:08.600
or a separate documentation,
where you get agreement

00:11:08.600 --> 00:11:10.600
you have with the developers
what the tiers mean

00:11:10.600 --> 00:11:12.590
and which service is
going to be in each tier.

00:11:12.590 --> 00:11:14.770
It's a way to help you to scale.

00:11:14.770 --> 00:11:17.380
So it kind of goes to-- you're
going from zero to one team,

00:11:17.380 --> 00:11:19.630
but you might be like, oh,
I feel like I need five SRE

00:11:19.630 --> 00:11:22.100
teams all at once.

00:11:22.100 --> 00:11:25.060
You may not need
to go from 0 to 5.

00:11:25.060 --> 00:11:27.085
Consider implementing
tiers of services.

00:11:30.710 --> 00:11:34.600
So people ask me about
team size and on-call.

00:11:34.600 --> 00:11:37.140
I'll just go through
this quickly.

00:11:37.140 --> 00:11:40.990
The gist of it is Google
has recommendations

00:11:40.990 --> 00:11:44.110
in the books about minimal
on-call size, et cetera.

00:11:44.110 --> 00:11:47.500
In reality, companies
tend to be a lot smaller,

00:11:47.500 --> 00:11:49.750
especially in the beginning
when they're adopting SRE,

00:11:49.750 --> 00:11:51.850
so we cannot really give
you a hard-and-fast rule,

00:11:51.850 --> 00:11:54.670
like here are the minimal for
every single implementation

00:11:54.670 --> 00:11:56.320
of SREs you should have.

00:11:56.320 --> 00:11:57.640
Consulting and embedded?

00:11:57.640 --> 00:11:59.800
You can start with
one SRE if you like.

00:11:59.800 --> 00:12:01.630
If that person is not
going to be on call,

00:12:01.630 --> 00:12:03.940
and there are no concerns
of burning that person out

00:12:03.940 --> 00:12:05.930
on call, you're totally fine.

00:12:05.930 --> 00:12:08.320
But if you have to
have 24/7 on-call,

00:12:08.320 --> 00:12:11.560
we do, at Google,
recommend every team that

00:12:11.560 --> 00:12:13.960
follows this set to have
a sister team, so six

00:12:13.960 --> 00:12:16.780
plus six at minimum,
or a total 12.

00:12:16.780 --> 00:12:18.220
So that's on the books.

00:12:18.220 --> 00:12:19.960
But back to it depends, right?

00:12:19.960 --> 00:12:21.460
It's a company thing.

00:12:21.460 --> 00:12:26.290
So I don't have a
set formula for you.

00:12:26.290 --> 00:12:28.540
Let's go over main risks of
each implementation, which

00:12:28.540 --> 00:12:30.610
I think can help you
if you're at zero team

00:12:30.610 --> 00:12:32.360
and you want to get
to your first team.

00:12:32.360 --> 00:12:34.840
So hopefully, that will help
you to decide what should

00:12:34.840 --> 00:12:36.620
be your first implementation.

00:12:36.620 --> 00:12:41.440
So for consulting, the more
consultants that you have,

00:12:41.440 --> 00:12:44.920
if you don't have anything
else, it may be perceived as--

00:12:44.920 --> 00:12:47.170
the team maybe
perceived as hands-off.

00:12:47.170 --> 00:12:49.360
Because you're telling
folks what to do,

00:12:49.360 --> 00:12:51.410
you're not doing it, right?

00:12:51.410 --> 00:12:53.620
So in the long run, the
more you have consultants,

00:12:53.620 --> 00:12:55.780
it's really hard to
scale their impact.

00:12:55.780 --> 00:12:57.917
They cannot really go
deep on the problem space.

00:12:57.917 --> 00:12:59.500
People are going to
be like, oh, yeah,

00:12:59.500 --> 00:13:01.950
the SRE consultants are
great, but we cannot have 50,

00:13:01.950 --> 00:13:05.560
100 consultants and keep adding
consultants indefinitely,

00:13:05.560 --> 00:13:07.090
depending on your company space.

00:13:07.090 --> 00:13:09.310
Back to if you have
a team of consultants

00:13:09.310 --> 00:13:11.440
and it's working well
for your company,

00:13:11.440 --> 00:13:14.480
you're measuring success however
you measure success internally,

00:13:14.480 --> 00:13:16.480
that's also fine.

00:13:16.480 --> 00:13:18.820
I'm just listing the
risks that I've seen based

00:13:18.820 --> 00:13:22.210
on my personal experience.

00:13:22.210 --> 00:13:25.690
Embedded may-- the individuals
in an embedded team

00:13:25.690 --> 00:13:28.150
may suffer from lack
of knowledge sharing.

00:13:28.150 --> 00:13:29.620
Let me tell you what that means.

00:13:29.620 --> 00:13:31.720
Several colleagues of my--
colleagues of mine in different

00:13:31.720 --> 00:13:33.280
companies, they tell me--
because they have several

00:13:33.280 --> 00:13:34.480
embedded teams--

00:13:34.480 --> 00:13:38.110
one common struggle
is they send one SRE--

00:13:38.110 --> 00:13:39.430
they have a team of 10 people.

00:13:39.430 --> 00:13:42.520
They send one SRE to 10
different developer teams,

00:13:42.520 --> 00:13:45.760
and it's really hard to get
those individual SREs back

00:13:45.760 --> 00:13:48.292
into a meeting room to
share their experience.

00:13:48.292 --> 00:13:49.000
So that's a risk.

00:13:49.000 --> 00:13:51.710
If you're not doing that, then
eventually those individual

00:13:51.710 --> 00:13:53.210
SREs that are
embedded on the teams,

00:13:53.210 --> 00:13:55.590
they feel more like such
a part of a developer team

00:13:55.590 --> 00:13:58.270
they're embedded with that
they struggle to come back

00:13:58.270 --> 00:14:00.522
and have this SRE
identity, which

00:14:00.522 --> 00:14:02.980
tends to be a problem because
they're not sharing their SRE

00:14:02.980 --> 00:14:04.247
experience with each other.

00:14:04.247 --> 00:14:05.830
So some things to
watch out for if you

00:14:05.830 --> 00:14:08.770
adopt an embedded strategy.

00:14:08.770 --> 00:14:11.470
For tools or infrastructure,
the only risk I've seen

00:14:11.470 --> 00:14:14.170
is the scope might be too narrow
for what the company needs,

00:14:14.170 --> 00:14:14.670
right?

00:14:14.670 --> 00:14:16.360
If your company has
several applications

00:14:16.360 --> 00:14:18.280
and you only have one
SRE team and their folks

00:14:18.280 --> 00:14:19.850
are doing tools
and infrastructure,

00:14:19.850 --> 00:14:22.405
it may be too narrow for them.

00:14:22.405 --> 00:14:27.180
For slice, it's similar to
tools, but one thing that--

00:14:27.180 --> 00:14:30.280
it may be obvious-- is if
you keep adding slice teams,

00:14:30.280 --> 00:14:33.730
it's really hard to scale just
by adding extra slice teams,

00:14:33.730 --> 00:14:35.390
SRE teams, to your company.

00:14:35.390 --> 00:14:37.510
And last but not
least, kitchen sink

00:14:37.510 --> 00:14:39.640
works really well in
the beginning, right?

00:14:39.640 --> 00:14:41.890
You have a team and their
scope is basically unbounded

00:14:41.890 --> 00:14:42.730
and it's everything.

00:14:42.730 --> 00:14:44.650
They can help out
with everything.

00:14:44.650 --> 00:14:46.870
As the company grows, as
the problem space grows,

00:14:46.870 --> 00:14:49.310
that team's impact
and their ability

00:14:49.310 --> 00:14:52.640
to understand things
deeply diminishes.

00:14:52.640 --> 00:14:54.940
So you see the deep
impact going from deep

00:14:54.940 --> 00:14:57.490
to very shallow impact.

00:14:57.490 --> 00:14:58.400
And why is that?

00:14:58.400 --> 00:15:01.440
Well, because there's just
too much work to do, right?

00:15:01.440 --> 00:15:05.327
So if the scope is unbounded,
one thing that we see

00:15:05.327 --> 00:15:06.910
is people start with
the kitchen sink,

00:15:06.910 --> 00:15:10.600
and then they eventually staff
consultants or embedded teams,

00:15:10.600 --> 00:15:13.250
or they actually start slicing
their kitchen sink into two,

00:15:13.250 --> 00:15:14.250
so they have two slices.

00:15:16.550 --> 00:15:17.270
Cool.

00:15:17.270 --> 00:15:19.120
So now let's go back
from implementations

00:15:19.120 --> 00:15:21.900
to common practice for most
implementations, right?

00:15:21.900 --> 00:15:24.940
I'll give you more concrete
examples based on maturity,

00:15:24.940 --> 00:15:28.180
and common practices I've seen
SRE teams adopting, and links

00:15:28.180 --> 00:15:30.550
to the book so then
you can kind of--

00:15:30.550 --> 00:15:34.870
as you're browsing the SRE
workbook at google.com/SRE,

00:15:34.870 --> 00:15:37.270
or one of the copies
that we have in the back,

00:15:37.270 --> 00:15:42.170
it'll make more sense for you
to do what I'm talking about.

00:15:42.170 --> 00:15:44.410
So beginner teams-- so
first thing's first, right?

00:15:44.410 --> 00:15:47.647
Staffing plan
should be in place.

00:15:47.647 --> 00:15:49.480
So how is it you're
going to fund this team?

00:15:49.480 --> 00:15:51.650
How many people do you need?

00:15:51.650 --> 00:15:55.085
So we talk about
this in the book.

00:15:55.085 --> 00:15:56.980
The team may or may
not be on call, right?

00:15:56.980 --> 00:15:57.920
So keep that in mind.

00:15:57.920 --> 00:16:01.810
You may have an implementation
that does not require on-call.

00:16:01.810 --> 00:16:05.860
On-call is something that
we say SRE does, may do.

00:16:05.860 --> 00:16:07.908
But several SREs are
not necessarily on call.

00:16:07.908 --> 00:16:09.700
So you don't need to
have your SREs on call

00:16:09.700 --> 00:16:11.350
to say I have an SRE team.

00:16:11.350 --> 00:16:14.630
That's not a requirement.

00:16:14.630 --> 00:16:17.680
We also generally
see beginner teams

00:16:17.680 --> 00:16:21.582
to start putting a release
process and canary rollback

00:16:21.582 --> 00:16:22.790
where it's applicable, right?

00:16:22.790 --> 00:16:24.332
So we understand
mobile applications,

00:16:24.332 --> 00:16:26.590
you may struggle
to have a canary,

00:16:26.590 --> 00:16:30.380
but you have a process to which
you may have a service side

00:16:30.380 --> 00:16:32.250
feature control.

00:16:32.250 --> 00:16:33.727
But generally
speaking, SREs tend

00:16:33.727 --> 00:16:35.310
to start being
concerned about release

00:16:35.310 --> 00:16:39.550
process in that beginner stage.

00:16:39.550 --> 00:16:42.090
A playbook or a [INAUDIBLE]
book, like the most common term

00:16:42.090 --> 00:16:44.622
is in place.

00:16:44.622 --> 00:16:46.580
And you do some kind of
disaster recovery task.

00:16:46.580 --> 00:16:49.020
So we also see, often,
beginner teams doing some kind

00:16:49.020 --> 00:16:50.405
of disaster recovery testing.

00:16:50.405 --> 00:16:52.030
It can be theoretical
in the beginning.

00:16:52.030 --> 00:16:54.890
You may not have software to
do any kind of quote, unquote

00:16:54.890 --> 00:16:58.150
"chaos generating activity."

00:16:58.150 --> 00:17:00.020
And SREs may be
doing, at that point,

00:17:00.020 --> 00:17:03.580
some kind of independent project
work in the beginner phase

00:17:03.580 --> 00:17:06.770
that may or may not be visible
by the developer counterparts.

00:17:06.770 --> 00:17:08.890
So this is important, right?

00:17:08.890 --> 00:17:11.079
In the very beginning,
SREs may be doing work

00:17:11.079 --> 00:17:14.260
to automate toil that
the developers may

00:17:14.260 --> 00:17:15.640
be even unaware of.

00:17:15.640 --> 00:17:18.082
My recommendation for
you is back to you

00:17:18.082 --> 00:17:19.290
got leadership buy-in, right?

00:17:19.290 --> 00:17:21.760
Remember the assumptions at
the beginning of the talk.

00:17:21.760 --> 00:17:25.540
Well, if you're just having
impact the developers cannot

00:17:25.540 --> 00:17:27.940
see, that your C-level
executives or your VPs cannot

00:17:27.940 --> 00:17:31.600
see, you may be doing great,
but this is bad, right?

00:17:31.600 --> 00:17:34.930
So you want to have some
kind of, eventually, project

00:17:34.930 --> 00:17:36.390
work that is going to be seen.

00:17:36.390 --> 00:17:38.463
The impact can be
measured, right?

00:17:38.463 --> 00:17:39.880
So in the beginner
phase, it's OK.

00:17:39.880 --> 00:17:42.310
You might be automating
toil for yourself.

00:17:42.310 --> 00:17:45.490
But I would also recommend you
to look into your first team.

00:17:45.490 --> 00:17:47.800
Are they doing the work
that possibly impacts

00:17:47.800 --> 00:17:49.930
the business that can
be measured and be

00:17:49.930 --> 00:17:52.400
seen by the rest of
the organization?

00:17:55.270 --> 00:17:58.030
Healthy SRE teams, regardless if
you're in the beginner stages,

00:17:58.030 --> 00:17:59.690
if you're just
really implementing

00:17:59.690 --> 00:18:05.080
SRE best practices at the top,
they have enough on-call load,

00:18:05.080 --> 00:18:08.500
meaning if you are
on-call, there are pages,

00:18:08.500 --> 00:18:10.588
but you're not on-call overload.

00:18:10.588 --> 00:18:12.130
We have a ton of
guidance in the book

00:18:12.130 --> 00:18:14.145
of what we think is
on-call overload at Google.

00:18:14.145 --> 00:18:16.270
What we think of what is
on-call overload at Google

00:18:16.270 --> 00:18:18.400
may be different
at your company.

00:18:18.400 --> 00:18:21.130
People might be more comfortable
with more pages, more alerts.

00:18:21.130 --> 00:18:22.990
People might be
comfortable with a lot less

00:18:22.990 --> 00:18:24.338
than we have at Google.

00:18:24.338 --> 00:18:25.630
You need to define that, right?

00:18:25.630 --> 00:18:28.450
You need to measure
that yourselves.

00:18:28.450 --> 00:18:31.030
And another super
important thing

00:18:31.030 --> 00:18:34.110
that should actually be
the title of this talk

00:18:34.110 --> 00:18:37.180
that I see in healthy SRE
teams is a team charter.

00:18:37.180 --> 00:18:39.370
So let me talk a little
bit about team charters.

00:18:39.370 --> 00:18:41.380
It's the constitution,
the definition

00:18:41.380 --> 00:18:43.030
of the scope of the team.

00:18:43.030 --> 00:18:45.142
Back to the kitchen
sink SRE team,

00:18:45.142 --> 00:18:47.350
if you just start an SRE
team and you don't have that

00:18:47.350 --> 00:18:49.750
document-- you're not
writing down your scope--

00:18:49.750 --> 00:18:50.780
your scope is unbounded.

00:18:50.780 --> 00:18:52.712
Your scope is
everything, and we hate

00:18:52.712 --> 00:18:53.920
that in software engineering.

00:18:53.920 --> 00:18:57.850
So why would we, as
SREs, accept, oh, sure,

00:18:57.850 --> 00:19:01.870
anything the company needs, in
terms of work that may or may

00:19:01.870 --> 00:19:04.480
not be related to
SRE, we will do?

00:19:04.480 --> 00:19:05.470
Define your boundaries.

00:19:05.470 --> 00:19:06.490
Define your scope.

00:19:06.490 --> 00:19:10.570
Sure, your boundaries may move
as the business needs move.

00:19:10.570 --> 00:19:12.712
But you probably want
to write this down.

00:19:12.712 --> 00:19:15.170
And it's better for everyone
to understand you're planning,

00:19:15.170 --> 00:19:17.990
to understand your work, to
understand your implementation,

00:19:17.990 --> 00:19:18.490
right?

00:19:18.490 --> 00:19:21.250
Is it kitchen sink, is it slice?

00:19:21.250 --> 00:19:23.570
And so on and so forth.

00:19:23.570 --> 00:19:26.650
And that gets me to you then--

00:19:26.650 --> 00:19:29.890
if you have a charter,
the discussions

00:19:29.890 --> 00:19:32.170
leading to writing
down a team charter

00:19:32.170 --> 00:19:34.560
gets you to have some kind
of SRE and have alignment.

00:19:34.560 --> 00:19:37.060
And that's when you're going
to see the misalignment, right?

00:19:37.060 --> 00:19:39.250
Things that you disagree
with your developer

00:19:39.250 --> 00:19:42.210
leadership, or your
C-level executives,

00:19:42.210 --> 00:19:45.490
or the priorities for production
and what the SLOs should look

00:19:45.490 --> 00:19:46.030
like.

00:19:46.030 --> 00:19:48.970
If people really buy
into not aiming for that

00:19:48.970 --> 00:19:52.060
unachievable 100% of uptime.

00:19:52.060 --> 00:19:54.310
And Joint planning
execution is also something

00:19:54.310 --> 00:19:55.630
that we see in healthy teams.

00:19:55.630 --> 00:19:59.350
So back to if your
developers, your executives,

00:19:59.350 --> 00:20:02.750
bought into yeah, we cannot
achieve 100% of uptime.

00:20:02.750 --> 00:20:04.990
The team charter for
the SRE team is great,

00:20:04.990 --> 00:20:09.790
and they are doing joint project
work with the developers.

00:20:09.790 --> 00:20:12.552
So all of a sudden,
yeah, SRE can

00:20:12.552 --> 00:20:14.260
be a successful
implementation of DevOps.

00:20:18.440 --> 00:20:20.430
Let's go to intermediate
SRE teams, right?

00:20:20.430 --> 00:20:21.893
So I covered it
in the beginning,

00:20:21.893 --> 00:20:24.560
and I covered a little bit about
what I see in health SRE teams,

00:20:24.560 --> 00:20:27.851
regardless if they're in
the beginner stages or not.

00:20:27.851 --> 00:20:32.180
Well, the SLO that
you set at the top

00:20:32.180 --> 00:20:35.060
is going to be reviewed
and changed, right?

00:20:35.060 --> 00:20:37.400
Generally, I recommend
every six months or so.

00:20:37.400 --> 00:20:39.660
But then again, whatever
works for your company.

00:20:39.660 --> 00:20:42.020
If you need a rule of
thumb, every six months,

00:20:42.020 --> 00:20:43.228
review your SLOs.

00:20:43.228 --> 00:20:45.020
The business priorities
might have changed.

00:20:45.020 --> 00:20:49.400
Your customers might be
more strict or less strict.

00:20:49.400 --> 00:20:52.250
So you want to review that
with your business owners.

00:20:52.250 --> 00:20:56.230
Review your metrics,
your objectives.

00:20:56.230 --> 00:20:58.570
We also see in the
intermediate teams,

00:20:58.570 --> 00:21:00.520
they start being able
to measure that they

00:21:00.520 --> 00:21:02.830
have some low volume of toil.

00:21:02.830 --> 00:21:06.997
By toil, meaning the work
that could be automated,

00:21:06.997 --> 00:21:08.830
or some operational
work that you don't want

00:21:08.830 --> 00:21:09.970
to automate at all.

00:21:09.970 --> 00:21:13.750
There are different definitions
of toil, I know, out there.

00:21:13.750 --> 00:21:15.170
It's a contentious issue.

00:21:15.170 --> 00:21:18.490
But the way you define
toil, we tend to say, well,

00:21:18.490 --> 00:21:21.820
half of the time for
an SRE team should

00:21:21.820 --> 00:21:24.530
be spent on software
engineering-related or system

00:21:24.530 --> 00:21:27.250
engineering-related
project work, right?

00:21:27.250 --> 00:21:29.950
Well, the other half
or less is then toil.

00:21:29.950 --> 00:21:30.930
Can you measure that?

00:21:30.930 --> 00:21:32.805
So intermediate SRE
teams tend to start being

00:21:32.805 --> 00:21:35.560
able to even measure that.

00:21:35.560 --> 00:21:38.050
We don't see that as much
at the beginner stages.

00:21:38.050 --> 00:21:41.870
The last point here
is you start reviewing

00:21:41.870 --> 00:21:44.390
incidents and the action
items of those incidents.

00:21:44.390 --> 00:21:47.860
Because you establish
blameless postmortem practices

00:21:47.860 --> 00:21:48.612
at the beginning.

00:21:48.612 --> 00:21:51.070
So now you start being able to
look at-- you have the time.

00:21:51.070 --> 00:21:53.770
You have the quote, unquote
"slack" for project work,

00:21:53.770 --> 00:21:56.260
for software engineering,
for systems engineering work.

00:21:56.260 --> 00:21:59.080
You're not drowning in toil or
in operational work anymore.

00:21:59.080 --> 00:22:01.580
Well, what is it that you
can do with the extra time?

00:22:01.580 --> 00:22:02.200
Well, one of the
things you can do

00:22:02.200 --> 00:22:03.880
with the extra
time is let's pause

00:22:03.880 --> 00:22:06.440
and evaluate the action items
that we have for incidents,

00:22:06.440 --> 00:22:09.430
and see if you prioritize
the right things to mitigate

00:22:09.430 --> 00:22:13.190
duplicate incidents.

00:22:13.190 --> 00:22:15.830
And at the end of the
day, SLO violations

00:22:15.830 --> 00:22:17.430
start to have consequences.

00:22:17.430 --> 00:22:18.608
So what does that mean?

00:22:18.608 --> 00:22:20.150
It doesn't necessarily
mean a freeze,

00:22:20.150 --> 00:22:22.550
even though we recommend
a freeze often.

00:22:22.550 --> 00:22:25.610
By freeze, meaning stop
launching new features

00:22:25.610 --> 00:22:29.510
if you completely exhaust your
SLO and invest in reliability.

00:22:29.510 --> 00:22:31.940
Several business that I work
with, they say, we can't.

00:22:31.940 --> 00:22:33.440
We can't afford to do that.

00:22:33.440 --> 00:22:35.220
What else can we do?

00:22:35.220 --> 00:22:38.270
Well, can you at least
dedicate more resources

00:22:38.270 --> 00:22:39.320
to improve reliability?

00:22:39.320 --> 00:22:41.810
If you cannot stop,
completely halt,

00:22:41.810 --> 00:22:45.470
your pushes for new features,
for new launches because you

00:22:45.470 --> 00:22:47.450
have contracts, you
have commitments,

00:22:47.450 --> 00:22:50.930
can you at least invest some of
the developers' time to improve

00:22:50.930 --> 00:22:51.620
reliability?

00:22:51.620 --> 00:22:54.590
Because at the end of
the day, if the product

00:22:54.590 --> 00:22:56.720
is completely down or
is not meeting the SLOs,

00:22:56.720 --> 00:22:58.250
the customers are leaving you.

00:22:58.250 --> 00:23:00.548
So SLO violations
have consequences.

00:23:00.548 --> 00:23:02.840
So that's one thing that is
common for intermediate SRE

00:23:02.840 --> 00:23:03.650
teams.

00:23:03.650 --> 00:23:07.010
The consequences, you have to
define it within your company.

00:23:07.010 --> 00:23:10.310
We do recommend pause
future launches,

00:23:10.310 --> 00:23:13.530
but I know it doesn't
always happen.

00:23:13.530 --> 00:23:16.070
Some kind of capacity
planning is in place, right?

00:23:16.070 --> 00:23:18.780
So if you're in the cloud,
if you're using Google Cloud,

00:23:18.780 --> 00:23:20.700
you understand
your quotas, you're

00:23:20.700 --> 00:23:24.330
working with your account team,
and you understand organic

00:23:24.330 --> 00:23:25.770
versus inorganic growth.

00:23:25.770 --> 00:23:27.990
You may or may not
have some software.

00:23:27.990 --> 00:23:30.280
SREs may have delegated
that to another team.

00:23:30.280 --> 00:23:32.640
But we see, often, SREs
being concerned about that.

00:23:32.640 --> 00:23:34.610
Some kind of capacity
planning is in place,

00:23:34.610 --> 00:23:39.543
and it could be purely quota
planning for Google Cloud.

00:23:39.543 --> 00:23:41.460
Last but not least for
intermediate SRE teams,

00:23:41.460 --> 00:23:43.690
I also see a lot of
strategic planning.

00:23:43.690 --> 00:23:45.900
So people start
writing road maps,

00:23:45.900 --> 00:23:50.640
thinking about not just the
next Scrum cycle, if you're

00:23:50.640 --> 00:23:53.190
on Scrum, or Agile,
or the next quarter.

00:23:53.190 --> 00:23:54.690
People are talking
about, what is it

00:23:54.690 --> 00:23:59.270
that we want to achieve by the
end of the year as an SRE team?

00:23:59.270 --> 00:24:03.380
Let's switch quickly to,
then, advanced SRE teams.

00:24:03.380 --> 00:24:04.940
Major positive business impact.

00:24:04.940 --> 00:24:06.710
At least one person,
two SREs that

00:24:06.710 --> 00:24:09.340
can claim that
because of their work,

00:24:09.340 --> 00:24:11.840
there is some major
positive business impact.

00:24:11.840 --> 00:24:15.560
Back to if your SLIs are
attuned with business metrics

00:24:15.560 --> 00:24:18.830
and SREs are working
to maintain or improve

00:24:18.830 --> 00:24:21.990
the business reliability,
well, it checks out, right?

00:24:21.990 --> 00:24:24.680
SREs should be able to claim
some major positive impact

00:24:24.680 --> 00:24:27.800
on the business.

00:24:27.800 --> 00:24:31.260
Scalable project
work, meaning SREs,

00:24:31.260 --> 00:24:34.230
if, in the intermediate
and beginner phases,

00:24:34.230 --> 00:24:38.810
they are trying to tackle one
service at a time, one problem

00:24:38.810 --> 00:24:41.870
space at a time,
they switch to now--

00:24:41.870 --> 00:24:46.830
or some parts of your team start
to switch to horizontal impact,

00:24:46.830 --> 00:24:49.130
meaning they don't work
just on one service with one

00:24:49.130 --> 00:24:50.073
team at a time.

00:24:50.073 --> 00:24:52.490
They're starting to come up
with solutions that positively

00:24:52.490 --> 00:24:55.430
impact several services,
several teams, several developer

00:24:55.430 --> 00:24:57.620
partners at once.

00:25:00.900 --> 00:25:02.672
SLO-burn-based
alerting is in place.

00:25:02.672 --> 00:25:04.130
This one here, I
would love to have

00:25:04.130 --> 00:25:07.400
that back in the beginners'
place, but I'm being realistic,

00:25:07.400 --> 00:25:08.510
right?

00:25:08.510 --> 00:25:11.900
Ideally, you would have that
for-- like every beginner SRE

00:25:11.900 --> 00:25:15.660
team that I see,
they have some kind

00:25:15.660 --> 00:25:17.150
of alerting that is SLO-based.

00:25:17.150 --> 00:25:19.770
I would be lying to you
if I said that's where

00:25:19.770 --> 00:25:22.400
I think the SRE practice is at.

00:25:22.400 --> 00:25:25.910
I think that's one that we
should move to the beginning,

00:25:25.910 --> 00:25:27.500
and we're not there yet.

00:25:27.500 --> 00:25:31.310
But if you can help me,
please, please, please

00:25:31.310 --> 00:25:33.290
work on SLO-burn-based alerting.

00:25:33.290 --> 00:25:34.520
And why is that?

00:25:34.520 --> 00:25:38.060
Because if you go look at your
alerts right now and you get

00:25:38.060 --> 00:25:41.060
alerted, there's probably
some impact to the system,

00:25:41.060 --> 00:25:44.090
but do you know if your
customers are unhappy?

00:25:44.090 --> 00:25:47.770
And are you measuring for how
much time they're unhappy?

00:25:47.770 --> 00:25:50.300
And back to linking to
Jesus's presentation,

00:25:50.300 --> 00:25:52.550
my colleague before,
about SLOs, right?

00:25:52.550 --> 00:25:54.900
You need to tune them.

00:25:54.900 --> 00:25:57.212
So if you don't have a
SLO-burn-based alerting,

00:25:57.212 --> 00:25:58.670
which kind of
alerting do you have?

00:25:58.670 --> 00:26:00.110
And are you happy
with your alerting?

00:26:00.110 --> 00:26:02.110
And are your customers
happy if you're alerting?

00:26:02.110 --> 00:26:04.700
Is it tuned with the
customer experience?

00:26:04.700 --> 00:26:08.000
The last one one is some kind
of automated disaster recovery

00:26:08.000 --> 00:26:09.020
testing.

00:26:09.020 --> 00:26:11.360
So then we see folks talking
about chaos engineering

00:26:11.360 --> 00:26:12.160
quite a bit, right?

00:26:12.160 --> 00:26:14.493
So it's something that I see
more in advanced SRE teams,

00:26:14.493 --> 00:26:17.417
where they understand
what is it that I

00:26:17.417 --> 00:26:19.250
can do that is theoretical
disaster recovery

00:26:19.250 --> 00:26:21.028
testing, or fault injection?

00:26:21.028 --> 00:26:22.820
And they understand
what is it that they're

00:26:22.820 --> 00:26:24.680
going to do in their
own production systems

00:26:24.680 --> 00:26:26.722
and what is it they're
going to do in production.

00:26:26.722 --> 00:26:29.570
And eventually, they have
some kind of automated system

00:26:29.570 --> 00:26:31.010
to stress test their SLO.

00:26:34.110 --> 00:26:34.610
Cool.

00:26:34.610 --> 00:26:36.485
So now we're at the
point in the presentation

00:26:36.485 --> 00:26:38.860
that people might be wondering,
what should I do next?

00:26:38.860 --> 00:26:40.210
What do I do from here?

00:26:40.210 --> 00:26:41.430
So first things first.

00:26:41.430 --> 00:26:43.140
Read the SRE workbook.

00:26:43.140 --> 00:26:45.900
We'll open for Q&amp;A shortly,
but if you're leaving,

00:26:45.900 --> 00:26:47.970
please get a copy
of the workbook.

00:26:47.970 --> 00:26:50.010
It's by the door now.

00:26:50.010 --> 00:26:51.690
And read about CRE.

00:26:51.690 --> 00:26:52.530
Read about my team.

00:26:52.530 --> 00:26:53.697
I have a link to the slides.

00:26:53.697 --> 00:26:56.438
The slides will be available
after the presentation.

00:26:56.438 --> 00:26:58.230
You can just google
Google CRE, and there's

00:26:58.230 --> 00:27:01.050
a whole blog post explaining
in details what we do.

00:27:01.050 --> 00:27:03.090
And contact your account
manager if you're

00:27:03.090 --> 00:27:05.430
interested in having
some version of this talk

00:27:05.430 --> 00:27:06.930
and also more
information about SLOs

00:27:06.930 --> 00:27:10.200
and instant response from
customer reliability engineers

00:27:10.200 --> 00:27:13.170
like myself, so SREs working
with you with the customers.

00:27:13.170 --> 00:27:15.270
So contact your account
manager about that.

00:27:15.270 --> 00:27:19.110
And right now, you can just
ask questions if you have any.

00:27:19.110 --> 00:27:22.460
[MUSIC PLAYING]

